(539467, 53825, 8125, '<td valign="top">Congrats on your top 10 finish.  This was one of my favorites.  I didn''t get a chance to vote - but it would''ve been one of my high scorers.  :-)  Great work and great idea...<br/></td>', 'Congrats on your top 10 finish.  This was one of my favorites.  I didn''t get a chance to vote - but it would''ve been one of my high scorers.  :-)  Great work and great idea...', '2004-01-12 09:13:09.000000', NULL, 0),
(539471, 53725, 8125, '<td valign="top">A most excellent kayaking shot... this would have been one of my top scoreres, but I didn''t get a chance to vote.  :-(<br/> <br/>My husband took up white water kayaking this past summer and sold his recreational kayak.  I''m keeping my rec boat... I''ll just photograph from the side.  :-)  Hard to believe this guy is in the water in Maryland in Dec/Jan.  Amazing!<br/> <br/>Congrats on a terrific photo.<br/></td>', 'A most excellent kayaking shot... this would have been one of my top scoreres, but I didn''t get a chance to vote.  :-( My husband took up white water kayaking this past summer and sold his recreational kayak.  I''m keeping my rec boat... I''ll just photograph from the side.  :-)  Hard to believe this guy is in the water in Maryland in Dec/Jan.  Amazing! Congrats on a terrific photo.', '2004-01-12 09:15:52.000000', NULL, 0),
(539472, 53756, 5112, '<td valign="top">Congratulations!!!  Excellent, I love how the lines of the tree in the background emphasize the "action" of this shot.<br/> <br/>Ursula</td>', 'Congratulations!!!  Excellent, I love how the lines of the tree in the background emphasize the "action" of this shot. Ursula', '2004-01-12 09:16:23.000000', NULL, 0),
(539479, 53672, 10985, '<td valign="top">Thank you all for your comments. It struck me that most people feel they have to know what they see to appreciate what it does to them, or even for it to do anything to them. This is a photo of a circus acrobat spinning on a long cord. I thought it gave the look of an angel. Next time I''ll be careful to enter a photo of something readily recognizable...</td>', 'Thank you all for your comments. It struck me that most people feel they have to know what they see to appreciate what it does to them, or even for it to do anything to them. This is a photo of a circus acrobat spinning on a long cord. I thought it gave the look of an angel. Next time I''ll be careful to enter a photo of something readily recognizable...', '2004-01-12 09:22:01.000000', NULL, 0),
(539502, 52844, 14509, '<td valign="top">Trying not to get your hopes up, aye. I''m glad you did. This is absolutely spendid. You took a great deal of thought composing this shot, and it shows.  Congratulations on your first place achievement.</td>', 'Trying not to get your hopes up, aye. I''m glad you did. This is absolutely spendid. You took a great deal of thought composing this shot, and it shows.  Congratulations on your first place achievement.', '2004-01-12 09:50:44.000000', NULL, 0),
(539506, 53734, 14509, '<td valign="top">Please accept my congratulations to yourself, and your son on achieving this place of honour.</td>', 'Please accept my congratulations to yourself, and your son on achieving this place of honour.', '2004-01-12 09:54:11.000000', NULL, 0),
(539548, 53768, 14614, '<td valign="top">Thanks for all your comments.  In all fairness to my subject, he was just warming up his service motion (therefore the ball in his hand)<br/> <br/>I learned a lot from taking the shots and from your comments. Collectively you outline the important aspects of action/sports photography.</td>', 'Thanks for all your comments.  In all fairness to my subject, he was just warming up his service motion (therefore the ball in his hand) I learned a lot from taking the shots and from your comments. Collectively you outline the important aspects of action/sports photography.', '2004-01-12 10:16:29.000000', NULL, 0),
(539632, 53643, 2780, '<td valign="top">Critique Club:<br/> <br/>Hi. First of all this photo fits the challenge, so there''s no problems there. The panning technique is one of the hardest to master and it''s very difficult to get decent results. I think you''ve done a good job here. I think the main reason that you didnt get a higher score is that even though the boy is sharp as far as the panning goes, he still looks a little soft. I think this is due to the post processing not the photo-taking. When you scale down a photo to fit DPC sizes, they always become soft. I''ve found the best way to fix this is to afterwards use Unsharp Mask with these variables:<br/> <br/>Amount: 170 %<br/>Radius: 0.4 pixels<br/>Threshold: 0 levels<br/> <br/>I tried this on your photo and it sharpens the boy a lot, improving the photo no-end.<br/> <br/>I think a slightly different crop would also make it a bit better. Perhaps moving the boy more towards the left of the frame. This would increase the space on the right of him, to make it look like he is riding <i>into</i> the photo. This is a technique used a lot for action, and also for portraits. Looking into the frame is often better than looking out of the frame.<br/> <br/>Overall this is a great attempt at the technique, and would look even better with just a little more basic post-processing.</td>', 'Critique Club: Hi. First of all this photo fits the challenge, so there''s no problems there. The panning technique is one of the hardest to master and it''s very difficult to get decent results. I think you''ve done a good job here. I think the main reason that you didnt get a higher score is that even though the boy is sharp as far as the panning goes, he still looks a little soft. I think this is due to the post processing not the photo-taking. When you scale down a photo to fit DPC sizes, they always become soft. I''ve found the best way to fix this is to afterwards use Unsharp Mask with these variables: Amount: 170 %Radius: 0.4 pixelsThreshold: 0 levels I tried this on your photo and it sharpens the boy a lot, improving the photo no-end. I think a slightly different crop would also make it a bit better. Perhaps moving the boy more towards the left of the frame. This would increase the space on the right of him, to make it look like he is riding into the photo. This is a technique used a lot for action, and also for portraits. Looking into the frame is often better than looking out of the frame. Overall this is a great attempt at the technique, and would look even better with just a little more basic post-processing.', '2004-01-12 10:57:10.000000', NULL, 0),
(539665, 52844, 14603, '<td valign="top">Thoroughly deserved.<br/>Another piece of great work</td>', 'Thoroughly deserved.Another piece of great work', '2004-01-12 11:44:36.000000', NULL, 0),
